  "query_preview": "..\nThrough next Thursday...\n\nPersistent above normal heights across the western CONUS will keep\nus warm & dry through at least the middle of next week...and \nlikely longer than that. The only chance of precip is associated \nwith a weak/splitting Pacific wave (currently over western ID) \nthat may bring a few light rain/snow showers to the Beartooth- \nAbsarokas this evening. \n\nThere are some foothills wind concerns (for the first time this \nfall) as we enter a period of tightened downslope gradients\ntonight to Sunday. In fact, we are already seeing reasonably high\ngusts today, with up to 43 mph at Livingston this morning. A\nbetter wind signature begins tomorrow. Expect periodic 35-45 mph\ngusts along the western foothills Friday & Saturday. Strongest\nwind potential which includes elevated gap flow is expected \nSaturday night into Sunday, with 30-40 knot winds at 700mb and a \nIDA-LWT gradient expected to exceed 12mb. Deepest pressure falls \nand much stronger upper level winds will exist well to our north \nnear the MT/AB border, but conditions along our foothills do \nsuggest potential for 50 mph gusts (60% chance) and a chance of 60\nmph gusts (30% chance) at Livingston & Nye, albeit somewhat \nbriefly. Sunday will be well-mixed and this will reduce gusts at \nthe gap areas and increase them (perhaps up to 50 mph) along the \nUS-191 corridor. A Sunday night fropa will reduce winds for Monday\nand beyond, with periodic easterly winds.\n\nAs already mentioned, temps will remain persistently warmer than\nnormal, and not by a little. Normal highs have fallen to the mid\n40s to lower 50s and we will likely see 50s/60s. The warmest day\nlooks to be the pre-frontal and well-mixed Sunday, with 700mb\ntemps surging to near +8C from Sheridan to Broadus. Temps on this\nday should be well into the 60s with some locations reaching the\nlower 70s. Daily records at our main climate stations are in the\nmid to upper 70s and we may not quite get that warm. We'll see. \nExpect 50s for Monday & Tuesday, but ensemble trends show 60s \nagain for the middle of next week.\n\nFor those looking for a pattern shift to something colder and\nsnowy, there are currently no signs. There is very high \nconfidence for warm & dry weather continuing through at least the\nfollowing weekend (Nov 15th). Stay tuned.\n\nJKL",
